WEBVTT

cue-VID_U4_01-1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:24.000
At Riverbend Bicycle Frames, welders finish 1,200 bicycle frames this month. Twenty production workers made them all. The owner wants one answer: is each worker producing more than last month? Watch frames stack on the rack, workers fade into the background, and a productivity meter rise above the factory floor. That question drives everything in this video.

cue-VID_U4_01-2
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:52.000
Labour productivity means output divided by labour input. At Riverbend, total output is 1,200 frames and labour input is 20 workers. Divide 1,200 by 20 and the answer is 60 frames per worker. The counter shows 1,200 frames pouring into one side and 20 worker icons lining the other, then sixty frames sliding into a single worker's column. Because output rose while the workforce stayed the same, unit labour cost falls.

cue-VID_U4_01-3
00:00:52.000 --> 00:01:20.000
Now compare two ways to organise work. In the labour-intensive workshop, 20 workers share 2 machines, a ratio of 10 workers per machine. In the capital-intensive line, 8 workers operate 16 machines, so the ratio is 1 worker per 2 machines. Watch the icons slide: worker tiles multiply on the left, machine arms multiply on the right. Capital intensity can lift output per worker, but each machine must be paid for.

cue-VID_U4_01-4
00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:48.000
Lean production aims to use fewer inputs and cut waste of materials, time and human effort. Riverbend orders tubing just in time, so stock is delivered when needed and storage space shrinks. Kaizen means continuous improvement through eliminating waste, with worker suggestions adjusting the jig each week. Watch stock piles melt, a delivery van sync with the line, and improvement loops turn. Therefore fewer resources are tied up, so unit costs fall.

cue-VID_U4_01-5
00:01:48.000 --> 00:02:16.000
Lean thinking names seven wastes. Overproduction stacks unsold frames; waiting leaves welders idle; transport drags parts across the floor; over-processing polishes hidden joints; inventory piles up; motion means reaching and walking; defects send frames back for rework. Each icon flashes red, then a green lean fix replaces it. Because every waste adds cost without adding value, removing one waste at a time raises productivity.

cue-VID_U4_01-6
00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:48.000
Three production methods suit three orders. Job production makes one bespoke item: a custom frame is measured, cut and welded alone. Batch production makes a quantity of one design, then switches to another: ten red frames, then ten blue. Flow production runs large volumes continuously: frames move down an automated line without stopping. Watch three assembly lines appear side by side, with a single unique frame, labelled groups, and a steady stream. Because volume and variety differ, the right method changes.

cue-VID_U4_01-7
00:02:48.000 --> 00:03:16.000
Technology changes how the frame is made. Computer-aided design, or CAD, lets engineers draw and modify the frame on screen. Computer-aided manufacturing, or CAM, sends that design to computer-controlled machines that cut and weld it. Automation keeps the line running with minimal human control. Watch the design rotate in three dimensions, travel as a digital file, and become a real frame. Output becomes more consistent, though software failures can stop everything.

cue-VID_U4_01-8
00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:44.000
Does lean production and technology always help? On one side, Riverbend cuts waste, raises output per worker and lowers unit costs. On the other side, just in time leaves no buffer if a supplier is late, and automation needs costly training. Watch a split screen: green cost markers fall on the left, risk icons flash on the right. Benefit: lower costs. Risk: supply delays and retraining.

cue-VID_U4_01-9
00:03:44.000 --> 00:04:12.000
Time to apply the idea. If Riverbend raises output to 1,440 frames with the same 20 workers, what is the labour productivity? Choose the calculation: divide 1,440 by 20, not by 2. The answer is 72 frames per worker, up from 60. Watch the counter divide the stack into twenty columns, then compare the new tile with the old marker to see the gain.

